SWEET AKS: a turn-off tells SpringBoard too, so the keypad goes away (2325b)

2325 made a set tell SpringBoard that a passcode exists: spc_lock_set raises SPC_EV_UNLOCKED as well as
SPC_EV_PASSCODE_CHANGED when a set leaves the session unlocked, and 0xd00d (AKSEvent 1) is what makes
SBFMobileKeyBag re-read the lock state. It missed the same change in the other direction. A turn-off runs
spc_lock_set with an empty code, `l->have_code` becomes false, and the guard `l->have_code && !l->unlocked` is
false, so only 0xd010 (AKSEvent 3) went out. SpringBoard's interest block returns on event 3 (0x1c16ec104), so its
cached has-passcode byte at +0x30 stayed 1 and the lock screen kept asking for a code that no longer existed until
SpringBoard restarted. On the phone that is a nuisance, not a lockout: any code opens it.

The mechanism, from the 26.6.2 cache (dcache.py, DSC_DIR on research/ios26/dsc):

  -_queue_handleKeybagStatusChanged (0x1c16ec1c4) is still the only writer of the byte outside init. It calls
  -_queue_lockStateExtended: (a fresh kernel read, selector 17) and then -_queue_setHasPasscodeIfNecessary:
  (0x1c16ec364), which is three instructions: objc_msgSend[lockState], `cmp x0,#3`, `cset w8,ne`, `strb w8,[x19,#0x30]`.
  So the byte is `sls != 3`, and once the code is gone the kernel already answers sls 3 (SPC_LS_NO_PASSCODE). The
  value is right; only the refresh is missing, which is the same defect 2325 fixed for a set.
  SBFMobileKeyBag's interest block (0x1c16ec08c) reaches that method only through AKSEvent 1, which is 0xd00d.
  AppleKeyStore.framework's chain (0x1e4ae45f4) maps 0xd00d -> 1 and 0xd010 -> 3, so 0xd010 cannot refresh the byte
  whatever the state says. Reading the event table again, 0xd00f is event 0 and its arm only sets +0x31 and calls
  -_queue_firstUnlockOccurred; it does not touch +0x30 either. 0xd00d is the only message that works, and it needs
  no argument: the framework passes the client a dictionary it builds from the kernel, never the message payload.

- SweetPasscode.c, spc_lock_set: a call that changes `have_code` in either direction now raises SPC_EV_UNLOCKED.
  `had_code` is read before the write, next to `first`, so the clear compares the state before the write with the
  state after it rather than with a value it has already changed. The clear also drops `l->unlocked`: with no code
  there is no open session to describe, and leaving it true would make the next set's guard fail, so a set after a
  turn-off would be silent and the byte would stay 0 on a phone with a brand-new code.
- SweetPasscode.h: the contract above, on spc_lock_set's declaration.
- SweetKeyStore.cpp: comments only, no code. The table and the event map are unchanged: the map already lists
  SPC_EV_UNLOCKED before SPC_EV_PASSCODE_CHANGED, so the 0xd00d of a turn-off goes out before its 0xd010. The
  comment block now names 0xd013 (event 6, the one type that does read its argument, which the kernel never sends),
  drops the wrong claim that 0xd00f reads its argument, and says what a turn-off sends and why.

The defect and this fix were both measured, not reasoned about. With `shim_msg_clear()` added before the turn-off,
2325's own `sb_turn_off` test fails at line 364 (`!kb.has_passcode`) with the one message logged being 0xe3ffd010,
AKSEvent 3. That is the audit's reproduction, and it is now a permanent control: check-2325.py rebuilds 2325's test
verbatim, puts 2325's clear back, and requires the run to PASS, so the blindness cannot come back unnoticed.

Host: check-2325.py runs springboard_test.c against the real SweetKeyStore.cpp. 76 assertions in 8 tests (was 59 in
7): the turn-off clears the log first and asserts it sends exactly 0xd00d then 0xd010 and that the byte's writer ran
once; a new `set off set` test proves the second set refreshes the byte again. 20 checks: 8 source, 11 negative
controls and the blindness control, every one caught. 2324's 147 two-phase and 89 selector checks are unchanged.

Not changed: the stub, every selector's arguments and answers, the two-phase stage and commit, the lock-state word,
the entitlement, root, and the count-before-hash ordering. 2325's set path is byte-for-byte what it was except for
the comment; the only new behaviour is on a call that changes `have_code` to false.

Needs 2321, 2322, 2323, 2323b, 2324 and 2325. Security code: plans/passcode/audit-brief-lock2.md is the audit brief
for the rebuilt image, and no image should boot until that audit is a GO.

